{
  "raw": {
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186181": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Chemistry, Tenure-track",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-30 Location: Granville OH The DEPARTMENT OF CHEMISTRY AND BIOCHEMISTRY at DENISON UNIVERSITY invites applicants for a full-time tenure-track position starting in fall 2027, with expertise in or related to analytical, computational, inorganic, or materials chemistry. Candidates should demonstrate a strong commitment to teaching at the undergraduate level and the ability to develop a robust, independent research program that recruits and engages undergraduates to produce published research. Teaching responsibilities include classroom and laboratory instruction at the introductory level, intermediate level (analytical, inorganic, physical, or polymer chemistry), and an advanced course in the candidate's field of expertise. Our department’s ACS-accredited program offers degrees in chemistry and in biochemistry and has excellent classroom and laboratory facilities with a broad range of available instrumentation for both teaching and research. See our website ( denison.edu/academics/chemistry-biochemistry ) for more information about our faculty, program, and facilities. Denison University is an academically rigorous and nationally ranked residential liberal arts college, located in the village of Granville, 30 minutes from Columbus, Ohio, the state capital. The thriving Columbus metropolitan area offers a wide range of opportunities for dual-career families, includes excellent school systems, and hosts an array of cultural and artistic events ( columbusregion.com ). Denison offers a competitive salary and a comprehensive benefits package. The college provides generous annual professional development funds, strong support for student and faculty research, a one-semester pre-tenure sabbatical, and a number of mentorship and professional development workshops and programs. Contributions to the life of the department, the institution, and the larger community are an essential part of fulfilling Denison’s mission, and the successful candidate is expected to engage in service activities in a way that is congruent with their work as a teacher-scholar. A Ph.D. and subsequent research experience are required. The priority date for full consideration is September 14, 2026. Applicants should submit the following: 1) a cover letter addressing their motivations for teaching at a small, residential, liberal arts college; 2) curriculum vitae, including a publication list; 3) undergraduate and graduate transcripts; 4) a statement of teaching philosophy addressing approaches to classroom and laboratory courses at the introductory and intermediate level, and a new course in their specialty; 5) a statement detailing the applicant’s proposed research program (5 page maximum). In their materials, applicants should discuss how their teaching and research mentorship will foster the engagement and success of students who have varying backgrounds and interests. Applicants are encouraged to include how they will incorporate rapidly evolving technological tools (such as data analytics, cheminformatics, machine learning, or generative AI), highlighting how such tools will be leveraged to benefit teaching and learning, research, or students’ career readiness and, if relevant, any existing or potential connections with industry, government, or non-profit sectors.",
      "department": "Chemistry & Biochemistry"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190829": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Sustainability & Environmental Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01 Location: Granville, Ohio The Denison University Sustainability & Environmental Studies (SES) program invites applications for a full-time tenure track position at the Assistant or Associate Professor level, beginning August 2027. We seek a colleague with an academic background in sustainability and environmental studies, a capacity for working across disciplinary boundaries, a commitment to supporting student learning in the liberal arts, and a specialty that complements the scholarly interests and teaching expertise of our current program faculty. Our program emphasizes experiential learning and robust community engagement. Through a broad array of liberal arts tools, we prepare students to be creative problem-solvers. Please see https://denison.edu/academics/sustainability-environmental-studies for more information. We are especially interested in candidates whose research and teaching advance innovation at the intersection of environmental sustainability, social change, and entrepreneurship. While we welcome applicants from all environmental specialties, we particularly seek teacher-scholars who take a social scientific approach to the study of organizational sustainability, environmental justice, sustainable development, sustainability analytics, law and policy, design, environmental education, or related areas, with a preference for a teaching and scholarly focus outside of the United States. We value interdisciplinary approaches that bridge theory, practice, and community engagement. Essential Job Functions The successful candidate will be capable of teaching all courses in the core of our curriculum: SES 100 Introduction to Sustainability & Environmental Studies; SES 200 Environmental Analysis; SES 301 Sustainability Practicum; and SES 401 Senior Project (see course descriptions at https://denison.edu/academics/sustainability-environmental-studies/courses) and advanced courses in the candidate’s area of expertise. The teaching load (typically 5 courses per year) will include sections of the core curriculum and advanced courses to meet the needs of the program. The successful candidate will also be a productive scholar who has demonstrated engagement in environmental sustainability initiatives with the broader community. Denison faculty are expected to maintain an active research agenda that results in scholarly publication and supports continued professional growth. The successful candidate will participate fully in the intellectual life of the SES program and the college through teaching, advising, scholarship, and service. Minimum Qualifications Candidates are required to have a Ph.D. in an appropriate discipline by August 2027. The successful candidate will: be an outstanding teacher who can teach effectively at all levels of our core curriculum; offer advanced courses that complement our current curricular strengths; be an active and engaging scholar; demonstrate a strong commitment to liberal arts education; and demonstrate the capacity to participate in a community-based interdisciplinary program. The successful candidate will have demonstrated expertise in environmental sustainability with an emphasis on innovation, social change, and/or entrepreneurship. Preferred Qualifications Previous teaching experience and a scholarly and teaching focus outside the United States are preferred but not required. Demonstrated community engagement in environmental sustainability initiatives is also of importance to the program. Applicants must submit their application materials via Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/192590 . Applications received by October 12, 2026 will receive full consideration. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Dossiers should include: a cover letter; CV; 2 page statement of teaching philosophy addressing the candidate's approach to environmental sustainability in a liberal arts setting; a 2 page statement on the candidate’s scholarly agenda; and the names and contact information for three professional references. Reference letters will be requested for top candidates at a later date.",
      "department": "Sustainability and Environmental Studies"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187936": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Mathematics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-27 Location: Granville, Ohio The Denison University Department of Mathematics invites applications for a three-semester visiting assistant professor appointment starting in the Spring of 2027. A PhD in Mathematics by the start date is preferred, but PhDs in related fields or candidates that are ABD will also be considered. In their cover letter, candidates should address their experience interacting with and mentoring undergraduate students. They should provide evidence of teaching excellence and the ability to create effective active learning experiences in the classroom. Prior experience teaching introductory calculus is preferred. Candidates also should speak to their ability to contribute to key components of Denison's mission “... to inspire and educate our students to become autonomous thinkers, discerning moral agents and active citizens of a democratic society.” Denison University is a diverse, highly selective, residential liberal arts college enrolling approximately 2,400 students from across the nation and around the world.  We emphasize building a supportive environment so that every student has the opportunity to succeed. We are located thirty miles from Columbus, Ohio, the state capital, which hosts many cultural and artistic opportunities. The Department of Mathematics graduates about 20 majors and 20 minors each year. In addition to a minor, BA, and BS in Mathematics, we also offer a minor and BS in Applied Mathematics. We engage our students in a myriad of ways ranging from department-sponsored social events to internally funded undergraduate research. We are also committed to mentoring our visiting faculty to be successful teachers, scholars, and colleagues as they progress in their careers. To apply, please upload a cover letter addressing this job description, a CV, a statement on your teaching philosophy, and a statement on your research program. Also ask three persons who know you well to upload reference letters in support of your application. At least two of these reference letters must address your teaching effectiveness and potential. Applications received by September 15 will receive full consideration, but we may continue to consider applications until the position is filled. A PhD in Mathematics by the start date is preferred, but PhDs in related fields or candidates that are ABD will also be considered. Prior experience teaching introductory calculus is preferred. Please upload a cover letter addressing this job description, a CV, a statement on your teaching philosophy, and a statement on your research program. Also ask three persons who know you well to upload reference letters in support of your application. At least two of these reference letters must address your teaching effectiveness and potential. Applications received by September 15 will receive full consideration, but we may continue to consider applications until the position is filled.",
      "department": "Mathematics"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186725": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Religion (Islam subfield; tenure track)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-01 Location: Granville, OH The Department of Religion at Denison University invites applications for an Assistant Professor (tenure-track) position in the Islamic studies subfield to begin in August 2027. We seek a broadly trained scholar of religion whose teaching and research engage diverse historical and geographic contexts within the Islamic tradition. Teaching responsibilities will include an introduction to the study of religion course, a survey of Islam, thematic seminars centered on Islamic traditions, and additional topical and/or theoretical courses that support the Religion major. The successful candidate will also teach writing-intensive courses that fulfill Denison’s writing program requirements. (More information about the writing program is available at http://denison.edu/academics/writing-program .) The standard teaching load is five courses per academic year. We seek an outstanding teacher-scholar who is committed to undergraduate education and thrives in the interdisciplinary environment of a residential liberal arts college. The Department of Religion is engaged in an exciting curricular restructuring and welcomes a colleague eager to help shape the future of the academic study of religion at Denison. Our curriculum emphasizes critical inquiry, close engagement with religious traditions, and the exploration of theoretical questions that illuminate religion across cultures and historical periods. We value teaching and advising that encourage intellectual curiosity, thoughtful dialogue, and engagement with diverse perspectives. The successful candidate will contribute to a vibrant, multivocal learning community and participate fully in the intellectual life of the department and the college through teaching, advising, scholarship, and service. Denison faculty are expected to maintain an active research agenda that results in scholarly publication and supports continued professional growth toward tenure. Denison is a highly selective, residential liberal arts college enrolling approximately 2,400 students from across the nation and around the world. The college is located in the village of Granville, 30 minutes from Columbus, Ohio, the state capital, which hosts a wide range of cultural and artistic opportunities. Granville offers an excellent public school system and easy access to outdoor activities. A supportive environment for teacher-scholars who value both classroom and research excellence, Denison offers extensive support for its faculty, including an annual professional development account, competitive professional development funding, and a one-semester research leave after a successful pre-tenure review.  The college also offers opportunities for student-faculty research collaboration, stipends for supervising summer-research students and a range of workshops and mentoring programs to develop our strengths as teacher-scholars. Denison offers a competitive salary and a comprehensive benefits package, including tuition benefits for eligible dependents to attend Denison or other affiliated colleges, and a generous retirement contribution. To be assured of full consideration, please apply by September 25th, when we will begin reviewing applications. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Questions can be directed to the search chair: Dr. Krista Dalton (daltonk@denison.edu). Ph.D. in Religion/Religious Studies, subfield Islam or related equivalent by start date of appointment in August 2027; potential for strong teaching and active research agenda in the field of Religious and Islamic Studies in a liberal arts college environment. Dossiers should include a cover letter; CV; 2 page statement of teaching philosophy addressing the candidate’s approach to teaching Islam and Religious Studies in a liberal arts setting; and three letters of reference. Syllabi will be requested from the top 10 candidates at a later date. In the application, candidates should provide evidence of teaching excellence and the ability to create effective active learning experiences in the liberal arts classroom. Candidates should further articulate a strong vision for their scholarship and anticipated trajectory for future research. To be assured of full consideration, please apply by September 25th, when we will begin reviewing applications. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Submit application via Interfolio at: https://apply.interfolio.com/188984 .",
      "department": "Religion"
    },
